<div style="font-family: Arial, sans-serif;">
    <h1 style="background-color: #1a5276; color: white; padding: 10px 20px; text-align: center; border-radius: 8px; margin-bottom: 20px;">
        Lesson Planner – Theme # ___ – Lesson # 1
    </h1>
    <p style="text-align: center; font-style: italic; margin-top: -10px; margin-bottom: 20px;">
        Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).
    </p>

    <table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 1</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Listening</td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><b>Grade:</b> Kinder (CEFR: A1.2)</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 25%;"><b>Scenario:</b> I'm Happy.</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 25%;"><b>Theme:</b> How Are You?</td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td colspan="3" style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Listening: Respond to simple oral requests.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Listening: Can recognize and respond to words related to emotions (e.g., 'happy,' 'sad,' or 'excited').
                Can follow instructions to express feelings, using gestures or facial expressions (e.g., Smiling in response to the prompt 'Show happiness.').
                Can participate in activities to identify or describe emotions when spoken (e.g., identifying emotions by pointing to the flashcard that represents the emotion when it is heard).</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
    </table>

    <h2 style="background-color: #1a5276; color: white; padding: 10px 20px; text-align: center; border-radius: 8px; margin-top: 30px; margin-bottom: 20px;">
        The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages
    </h2>

    <table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
        <thead>
            <tr style="background-color: #f2f2f2;">
                <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 30%;">Stage & Description</th>
                <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
                <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 20%;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
            </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
            <tr>
                <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                    <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</h3>
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Engagement, Modeling and Clarification.</p>
                </td>
                <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher greets students with a "Hello Song" that includes simple actions for 'happy' and 'sad.' The teacher models the actions and sings, encouraging students to listen and imitate. For example, "Hello, hello, how are you? I'm happy!" (smile, thumbs up).</p>
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher holds up large flashcards displaying different emotions (happy, sad, excited, tired). For each flashcard, the teacher clearly says the emotion word, models the corresponding facial expression and gesture, and forms a simple sentence: "I'm happy." "I'm sad." "I'm excited." (emphasizing /h/ in 'happy' and /e/ in 'excited'). The teacher then models how to respond to "How are you?" by saying "I'm happy!" with a gesture.</p>
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b> The teacher checks understanding of the target vocabulary and simple questions by asking:
                    <ul style="margin-left: 20px; padding: 0;">
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">(Showing a sad face) "Am I happy?" (Students listen and shake heads or say "No.")</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">(Pointing to the 'happy' flashcard) "Is this happy?" (Students listen and nod or say "Yes.")</li>
                    </ul>
                    </p>
                </td>
                <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">10 min</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                    <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 2 — Presentation</h3>
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Input of new language/content connected to Scenario: "I'm Happy."</p>
                </td>
                <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> Large, colorful picture flashcards of 'happy,' 'sad,' 'excited,' 'tired.' Simple audio story or teacher-read dialogue featuring characters expressing these emotions. </p>
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Expressing feelings and needs by saying, 'I'm happy.' or 'I'm tired.'; Responding to questions about feelings by answering 'How are you?' with simple 'I'm...' statements (e.g., 'I'm excited.'); Discussing feelings and recognizing others' feelings.</p>
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b></p>
                    <ul style="margin-left: 20px; padding: 0;">
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher plays or reads a short dialogue/story, "The Feelings of My Friend," where two friends (e.g., 'friend' noun) talk about their feelings. Example: "Hi, friend! How are you? I'm happy! My friend says, 'I'm excited!'"</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Gist question:</b> After listening to the story, the teacher asks, "What feeling is the boy?" and "What feeling is the girl?" Students listen and point to the corresponding flashcards.</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Listen and Point:</b> The teacher calls out a character from the story and an emotion (e.g., "Listen: Friend is sad."), and students point to the correct emotion flashcard.</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>True/False (Gestures):</b> The teacher says a sentence from the story (e.g., "The friend is happy!") and shows a sad face. Students listen and respond with a thumbs up for true or thumbs down for false.</li>
                    </ul>
                </td>
                <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">15 min</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                    <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</h3>
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Accuracy focus on Listening.</p>
                </td>
                <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b>
                    <ul style="margin-left: 20px; padding: 0;">
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Grammar:</b> Simple sentences ('I'm happy.'), WH-questions ('How are you?'), I'm + adjective ('I'm excited.').</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Vocabulary:</b> happy, sad, tired, excited, friend, smile, frown.</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Pronunciation:</b> /h/ in 'happy,' /e/ in 'excited.'</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Phonemic Awareness:</b> Recognizing letters of the alphabet in familiar words (e.g., 'H' in 'happy,' 'E' in 'excited').</li>
                    </ul>
                    </p>
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b></p>
                    <ul style="margin-left: 20px; padding: 0;">
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>1. Listen and Point Game:</b> The teacher places emotion flashcards around the classroom. The teacher calls out an emotion (e.g., "Everyone, point to 'sad'!"). Students listen and quickly point to the correct flashcard. This is repeated several times with various emotions. The teacher subtly introduces phonemic awareness: "Sad starts with S-S-S... S!" (Teacher writes 'S' on the board).</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>2. Listen and Show (TPR):</b> The teacher gives oral instructions: "Show happy!" or "Show tired!" (using 'tired' vocabulary). Students listen and use facial expressions and gestures to physically demonstrate the feeling (e.g., yawning for tired). The teacher reminds them to "smile" for happy and "frown" for sad.</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>3. Listen and Color:</b> Students are given a worksheet with simple outlines of faces. The teacher instructs: "Color the happy face green!" or "Color the sad face blue!" Students listen to the color and emotion and color the correct face. (Teacher may also ask: "What letter does 'Happy' start with?" and show the letter 'H').</li>
                    </ul>
                </td>
                <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">10 min</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                    <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</h3>
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario: "I'm Happy."</p>
                </td>
                <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Format:</b> "Feeling Circle" – a guided interaction where students listen and respond to the question "How are you?" using gestures and simple verbal cues.</p>
                    <ul style="margin-left: 20px; padding: 0;">
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students sit in a circle. The teacher starts by asking a student, "How are you, [Student's Name]?"</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The student listens and responds by showing a facial expression (e.g., a smile for happy, a frown for sad) and, if comfortable, a one-word answer (e.g., "Happy!").</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher then guides that student to ask the next student, "How are you?" (or the teacher facilitates the question, emphasizing listening to the name).</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The activity continues around the circle, reinforcing the listening comprehension of the question and expressing emotions. The teacher encourages natural expressions and may prompt with specific vocabulary: "Are you excited?" if a student looks energetic.</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">(Optional: Use a "feeling ball" with emotion pictures. The teacher throws the ball and asks, "How are you?" The student catches it, points to a feeling on the ball, and shows the face.)</li>
                    </ul>
                </td>
                <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">8 min</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                    <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</h3>
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Formative assessment strategy.</p>
                </td>
                <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative Assessment Strategy:</b> Teacher observation checklist during the "Feeling Circle" and "Listen and Respond" activity.</p>
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> "Listen and Respond to the Feeling." The teacher individually calls out students or shows a flashcard. The teacher says, "Show me happy!" or "How are you?" and students respond with an appropriate facial expression and/or gesture, or by pointing to the correct flashcard/picture. The teacher listens for their recognition and response.</p>
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria:</b>
                    <ul style="margin-left: 20px; padding: 0;">
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Accurate response to oral requests (e.g., showing the correct emotion or pointing).</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Clarity of gestures/facial expressions in conveying the emotion.</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">(If speaking) Intelligible pronunciation of single target words (e.g., "happy").</li>
                    </ul>
                    </p>
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> Students are given a small paper with a happy face and a sad face. The teacher asks, "Did you understand the feeling words today?" Students circle the happy face if they understood well, or the sad face if they found it difficult.</p>
                </td>
                <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">7 min</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                    <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 6 — Reflection</h3>
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Review and planning for future lessons.</p>
                </td>
                <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Reflection:</b> The teacher asks the students:
                    <ul style="margin-left: 20px; padding: 0;">
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What new feeling did you learn to show today?" (Students listen and point to flashcards or show faces).</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"How do you feel right now?" (Students respond with a gesture or simple word).</li>
                    </ul>
                    </p>
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher Reflection:</b>
                    <ul style="margin-left: 20px; padding: 0;">
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What worked well in engaging Kinder students with listening activities (e.g., TPR, songs, flashcards, story)? The "Listen and Show" game was very effective.</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What adjustments or additional scaffolds are needed for the next lesson (e.g., more repetition of "How are you?" for passive listening, clearer visual cues for word recognition)? I need to ensure the vocabulary for 'tired' and 'excited' is as strong as 'happy' and 'sad'.</li>
                    </ul>
                    </p>
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson in the theme sequence:</b> In the next lesson (Reading), students will encounter these same emotion words in a simple illustrated text. The strong listening foundation will help them recognize the words visually and connect them to meaning. We will transition from hearing and showing to hearing and seeing the words.</p>
                </td>
                <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">5 min</td>
            </tr>
        </tbody>
    </table>